PROGRAM: compareTrees

USAGE: compareTrees [OPTIONS] estimate.nwk reference.nwk > output.txt

DESCRIPTION: Compare two collections of phylogenetic trees representing
samples from two posterior (or other) distributions over tree space --
for example, samples from a variational approximation and from an MCMC
reference run.  By default, reports the topology-based mean split KL
divergence.  Additional comparison metrics can be requested with options.

(1) Mean split KL: the mean per-split Bernoulli KL divergence between the
two samples' posterior distributions over clade splits.  For each
non-trivial clade split observed in either estimate.nwk or reference.nwk,
treats inclusion of the split as a Bernoulli variable with posterior
inclusion probability estimated from the corresponding sample, and
computes KL(p_est(s) || p_ref(s)).  The reported value is the average over
the union of splits observed in either sample (Laplace-smoothed so no
split has probability exactly 0 or 1).  This approximates the KL
divergence between the two posterior distributions over tree topology,
and remains well-behaved even when the two samples share few or no exact
topologies, unlike the full-topology KL divergence -sum p(tau) log
(p(tau)/q(tau)).

(2) Mean pairwise-distance KL (enabled by --pairwise-dist-kl): for each
leaf pair, a Gaussian(mean, sd) is fit to its raw tree distance across each
sample.  The reported value is the mean Gaussian KL divergence over leaf
pairs with nonzero variance in both samples.

(3) Mean embedding KL (enabled by --embed-dist-kl): each tree is independently embedded into a
low-dimensional Euclidean space via classical multidimensional scaling on
its cophenetic distances -- the same closed-form procedure vine itself
uses to initialize its variational posterior (see vine's
--dist-embedding option).  Pairwise Euclidean distances between the
embedded leaves are computed for each tree (these are invariant to the
arbitrary rotation/reflection/translation of each independent embedding,
so no alignment across trees is needed).  A Gaussian(mean, sd) is fit to
each leaf pair's embedded distance across the sample, and the reported
value is the mean Gaussian KL divergence over all leaf pairs.

Both input files must contain one Newick tree per line, sampled over the
same set of taxa.

OPTIONS:
    --pairwise-dist-kl, -p
        Also compute the mean Gaussian KL divergence between the raw
        pairwise leaf-distance distributions.  This comparison is disabled
        by default.

    --embed-dist-kl, -e
        Also compute the mean embedding-distance KL divergence.  This
        comparison is disabled by default.

    --dim, -d <n>
        Embedding dimensionality used for the embedding KL divergence.
        Defaults to the same formula vine uses when --dimensionality is
        not specified (based on the number of taxa).

    --nthreads, -n <n>
        Number of threads used by OpenBLAS for the eigendecompositions
        underlying the embedding KL divergence.  Defaults to 1, matching
        vine's own default.

    --help, -h
        Print this help message and exit.
